WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:04.523
The search for peace is probably as old as the history of human conflict. Today, citizens in

2
00:00:04.524 --> 00:00:10.176
every nation work toward harmony even as wars rage around the globe. Will lasting peace ever be

3
00:00:10.177 --> 00:00:16.509
achieved? Can all the countries of the world ever unite to create peace and security? In 1945,

4
00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:25.444
51 nations joined to create an international organization to promote human rights, justice, and above all, world

5
00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:27.849
peace. It was called the United Nations. Membership in the United Nations, or UN, is voluntary,

6
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:34.380
and almost every country in the world has joined. These member nations pay dues to the UN

7
00:00:34.381 --> 00:00:38.386
to fund its activities. By 2007, 192 countries had joined. Nations had tried once before to create

8
00:00:38.387 --> 00:00:42.545
an international organization to safeguard world peace. In reaction to the twenty million deaths caused by World

9
00:00:42.546 --> 00:00:48.091
War I, its victors formed the League of Nations. But the organization was unable to prevent World

10
00:00:48.092 --> 00:00:54.161
War II only twenty years later. Some nations were nearly destroyed by the Second World War, but

11
00:00:54.162 --> 00:00:58.866
others became stronger because of it. Among them, the United States of America, Russia, Great Britain, France,

12
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:04.745
and China came to be known as "superpowers." They led the way in the creation of the

13
00:01:04.746 --> 00:01:12.078
United Nations. In October of 1945, the 51 founding members of the UN signed a charter, agreeing

14
00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:15.302
to follow a set of rules and principles for peaceful cooperation between nations. Every country, no

15
00:01:15.303 --> 00:01:28.054
matter how small or how poor, was important to the UN. To bring peace and prosperity to

16
00:01:28.055 --> 00:01:33.283
all the corners of the globe, the organization needed to be huge. Six distinct organs were created

17
00:01:33.284 --> 00:01:38.836
for the UN to do its work. The General Assembly is the forum in which each member

18
00:01:38.837 --> 00:01:42.492
of the UN has a voice and a vote. At the UN headquarters in New York City,

19
00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:47.988
members raise issues for discussion. The General Assembly makes recommendations to solve them, but it has no

20
00:01:47.989 --> 00:01:52.875
power to enforce their resolutions. Debate in the General Assembly does, however, sometimes attract the world’s attention

21
00:01:52.876 --> 00:01:58.984
to important issues, such as the rise of HIV/AIDS. In this way, the General Assembly has forced

22
00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:07.477
reluctant nations to deal with matters they might otherwise have ignored. The Security Council is the most

23
00:02:07.478 --> 00:02:14.652
powerful organ in the UN. Its 15 members are responsible for maintaining international peace and security.

24
00:02:14.653 --> 00:02:18.291
The superpowers that worked together to create the UN are permanent members. Ten nonpermanent members from around

25
00:02:18.292 --> 00:02:22.963
the globe are elected every two years. The five permanent members of the Security Council have veto

26
00:02:22.964 --> 00:02:27.182
power, meaning any one of them can block any action the rest of the council has authorized.

27
00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:31.560
The Security Council presses nations to resolve disputes peacefully, but if necessary, it can authorize military action,

28
00:02:31.561 --> 00:02:40.274
using troops offered by member states. In 1950, when North Korean troops attacked South Korea, the Security

29
00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:50.171
Council sent troops from 16 member nations to fight beside the South Korean army. The cease-fire signed

30
00:02:50.172 --> 00:02:59.604
in 1953 exists to this day. The other four organs of the UN work around the clock

31
00:02:59.605 --> 00:03:03.760
and around the world every day. The Secretariat is the office of the secretary-general of the United

32
00:03:03.761 --> 00:03:08.494
Nations. Its nine thousand employees oversee the daily work of the UN. The Economic and Social

33
00:03:08.495 --> 00:03:13.143
Council coordinates the work of the UN and hundreds of nongovernmental organizations (NGOs) that work on cultural

34
00:03:13.144 --> 00:03:16.853
and humanitarian issues, such as human rights and drug control. The International Court of Justice decides disputes

35
00:03:16.854 --> 00:03:23.795
between nations and advises the UN on international law. The Trusteeship Council is now inactive, but prior

36
00:03:23.796 --> 00:03:29.525
to 1994, it oversaw former colonies as they moved toward independence; for instance, it aided the many

37
00:03:29.526 --> 00:03:36.452
African territories once under French control to become independent nations. Many specialized agencies and NGOs assist the

38
00:03:36.453 --> 00:03:43.897
UN. Among them, the UN Educational, Scientific, and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) works to provide basic education for

39
00:03:43.898 --> 00:03:50.387
all people. The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) strives to control the development of nuclear weapons and

40
00:03:50.388 --> 00:03:54.671
ensure that nuclear power plants are operated safely. The World Health Organization (WHO) devotes itself to fighting

41
00:03:54.672 --> 00:04:03.385
disease and improving health throughout the world. The United Nations has met with both success and

42
00:04:03.386 --> 00:04:14.088
failure during its existence. Since the creation of the United Nations, a third world war has not

43
00:04:14.089 --> 00:04:21.142
taken place, and many problems have been resolved. In 1967, the WHO started a program to eradicate

44
00:04:21.143 --> 00:04:25.648
smallpox, a disease that had caused millions of deaths over hundreds of years. By the end of

45
00:04:25.649 --> 00:04:31.971
the seventies, smallpox had become the only disease in the history of humanity to have been completely

46
00:04:31.972 --> 00:04:41.576
eliminated. And in 1995, UN peacekeeping forces ended a five-year civil war in the Central American nation

47
00:04:41.577 --> 00:04:48.337
of El Salvador. The world has not, however, been freed from war, social injustice, poverty, or disease.

48
00:04:48.338 --> 00:04:56.823
In 1969, the World Health Organization announced a plan to eliminate the tropical disease malaria from the

49
00:04:56.824 --> 00:05:02.338
world. Although some areas have seen success, in others, such as central and southern Africa, the rate

50
00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:09.424
of malaria infection has only increased. The UN was also unable to prevent genocide in Rwanda,

51
00:05:09.425 --> 00:05:17.987
a country in Africa. In 1994, people from the Hutu group began to murder people from the

52
00:05:17.988 --> 00:05:23.312
Tutsi group. The UN withdrew its small peacekeeping force to protect the troops from the violence, and

53
00:05:23.313 --> 00:05:28.651
over 800,000 people died in just three months. Even as more countries join the United Nations, it

54
00:05:28.652 --> 00:05:36.643
continues to be the subject of controversy. Many members complain about the power of the Security Council.

55
00:05:36.644 --> 00:05:42.141
The number of permanent members has not increased since 1945 despite changes in the status of individual

56
00:05:42.142 --> 00:05:45.685
countries. For instance, Japan and Germany have become powerful and wealthy since their defeat in World War

57
00:05:45.686 --> 00:05:52.906
II, but are still denied permanent seats. Others fear that the UN is paving the way for

58
00:05:52.907 --> 00:06:01.503
one world government, with the superpowers in control and the poorer nations having little say in important

59
00:06:01.504 --> 00:06:11.441
decisions. Every member of the UN has the right to govern its territory without other countries’

60
00:06:11.442 --> 00:06:17.108
interference. The Security Council can, however, force members to take action for the greater good of all—for

61
00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:27.872
example, it can make members halt trade with a country even if they disagree with the decision.

62
00:06:27.873 --> 00:06:36.715
The conflict between the right to self-rule and the need to comply with the demands of the

63
00:06:36.716 --> 00:06:40.801
Security Council sometimes causes suspicion and anxiety. Members of the United Nations also disagree about its long-term

64
00:06:40.802 --> 00:06:50.626
future. Some think the UN should concentrate on humanitarian issues like fighting poverty and disease, while others

65
00:06:50.627 --> 00:06:56.903
think it should focus on global politics and peacekeeping. When the United Nations was created in 1945,

66
00:06:56.904 --> 00:07:01.336
it was intended to be a champion of world peace. More than sixty years later, the understanding

67
00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:07.899
of world peace may have changed. Humankind has often turned to weapons and war, but poverty and

68
00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:13.207
disease can also lead to violent uprisings, and a lack of education and cultural knowledge can

69
00:07:13.208 --> 00:07:19.395
breed deep resentments between nations. In 2000, the UN developed eight goals it wanted to meet by

70
00:07:19.396 --> 00:07:24.329
2015, called the Millennium Goals. These include cutting world poverty in half and ending the spread of

71
00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:29.040
HIV/AIDS. The Millennium Goals reflect just how much the UN’s mission has changed. As the world continues

72
00:07:29.041 --> 00:07:35.746
its search for true and lasting peace, perhaps the United Nations is now more important than ever.

